[{"id":56847,"sender_id":450,"sender_full_name":"lentic catachresis","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/5439655312d444a69cfa66507b631194?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/Homological.20functors/near/56847\">#1</a> Hi,</p>\n<p>I'd love to see a bit more details at the \"homological functor\" page. I'm not well-versed in infinity-language, but it seems to me that the examples there cover a variety of classical ones, like: stable homotopy (first example), chain homology (third example), and I guess that for a spectrum E, E-homology on spaces or on spectra also fits there somehow?</p>\n<p>Thanks!</p>","timestamp":1462885651,"subject":"Homological functors","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":56850,"sender_id":11,"sender_full_name":"Urs","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/b00d1ee17463af676c78974439b92471?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/Homological.20functors/near/56850\">#2</a> Yes, generalized homology theories are examples. For a little exposition that relates the traditional way of stating this to the <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\infty</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∞</span></span></span></span>-categorical way see <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Introduction+to+Stable+homotopy+theory%20--%20S#GeneralizedHomologyAndCohomologyFunctors\">here</a> (go down up to prop. 1).</p>","timestamp":1462895083,"subject":"Homological functors","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":56851,"sender_id":450,"sender_full_name":"lentic catachresis","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/5439655312d444a69cfa66507b631194?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/Homological.20functors/near/56851\">#3</a> Thanks for the link! In definition 4, isn't C lacking a point in item 1?</p>","timestamp":1462896428,"subject":"Homological functors","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":56852,"sender_id":11,"sender_full_name":"Urs","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/b00d1ee17463af676c78974439b92471?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/Homological.20functors/near/56852\">#4</a> Thanks, yes, there was a pointedness-symbol missing. I have fixed it. Thanks.</p>","timestamp":1462898863,"subject":"Homological functors","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":123513,"sender_id":17,"sender_full_name":"zskoda","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/0ea9c6065e45ce1c059bd086dadd928e?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/Homological.20functors/near/123513\">#5</a> I am familiar with (co)homological functors in the setup of triangulated categories. Simply, a distinguished triangle X-&gt;Y-&gt;Z-&gt;Sigma X by 1-functoriality on the first three terms creates a sequence  HX-&gt;HY-&gt;HZ which is required to be exact at HY. This is all. Then by shift, using axiom TR2, one can get long exact sequence.</p>\n<p>Now the definition here is imprecise, what does it mean that infinity functor “transforms” 4-term sequence into infinity term and the line with infinite sequence does not have an index n while there is a definition of a subscript the line before. I do not know how to fix it as I know the concept just in triangulated context.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/homological%20functor\">homological functor</a></p>\n<p>P.S: I could resolve this myself, but I am not sure if I would do it the desired and optimal way, so I am rather posting a friendly query.</p>","timestamp":1769185542,"subject":"Homological functors","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":123517,"sender_id":563,"sender_full_name":"Hurkyl","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ff42efa94f111c3f310350c872de031d?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/Homological.20functors/near/123517\">#6</a> In an ∞-category with a zero object and finite colimits, distinguished tringles are essentially uniquely determined simply by the leading arrow <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X \\to Y</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">Y</span></span></span></span>,  since a <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/cofiber%20sequence\">cofiber sequence</a> is just a pushout (which is essentially unique) against <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X \\to *</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4653em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∗</span></span></span></span> (which is also essentially unique).</p>\n<p>So by pasting these pushout sequences together along their shared sides, you get a diagram that contains, among other things a path <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><mo>…</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X \\to Y \\to Z \\to \\Sigma X \\to \\ldots </annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">Y</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">Z</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">Σ</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.123em;\"></span><span class=\"minner\">…</span></span></span></span>. Applying a functor <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">π</span></span></span></span> thus gives a diagram <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>→</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>→</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>→</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>→</mo><mo>…</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi(X) \\to \\pi(Y) \\to \\pi(Z) \\to \\pi(\\Sigma X) \\to \\ldots </annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">π</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">π</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">Y</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">π</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">Z</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">π</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">Σ</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.123em;\"></span><span class=\"minner\">…</span></span></span></span>. (and the rest of the diagram shows this is a complex, if <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">π</span></span></span></span> preserves the zero object)</p>\n<p>Just to be explicit: in the ∞-category version of the theory, the arrow <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X \\to Y</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">Y</span></span></span></span> really is all you need to essentially uniquely determine the long cofiber sequence, and the rest of the diagram <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y \\to Z \\to \\Sigma X</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">Y</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">Z</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">Σ</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span></span></span></span> is redundant*. This is one of the advantages it has over theory based on triangulated categories.</p>\n<p>Dually, with finite limits you can construct <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/fiber%20sequence\">fiber sequence</a>s which extend to long sequences in the other direction. And in a stable ∞-category pushout squares are pullback squares, so fiber and cofiber sequences are the same thing and you can extend in both directions.</p>\n<p>Does this help?</p>\n<p><em>: As an additional aside, if I recall correctly, in the ∞-category version, the diagram <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X \\to Y \\to Z \\to \\Sigma X</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">Y</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">Z</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">Σ</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span></span></span></span> is lacking information that really ought to be included for a lot of purposes, such as the specific choice of nullhomotopies showing <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X \\to Z</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">Z</span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ</mi><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y \\to \\Sigma X</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">Y</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">Σ</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span></span></span></span> are zero morphisms (e.g. as would be provided by the relevant pushout squares). Or that it should be a diagram whose shape is something representing a </em>complex* rather than merely a path with three arrows.</p>\n<p>But mapping into the 1-category <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span></span></span></span> is not one of those purposes, since mapping into a 1-category forgets that sort of information.</p>","timestamp":1769229439,"subject":"Homological functors","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":123520,"sender_id":11,"sender_full_name":"Urs","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/b00d1ee17463af676c78974439b92471?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/Homological.20functors/near/123520\">#7</a> I have now made mild edits to the entry, for better clarity, see the announcement <a href=\"#narrow/channel/2/topic/homological.20functor/near/123519\">there</a>.</p>","timestamp":1769268288,"subject":"Homological functors","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]}]